package cosmonic:agent@0.5.0;

/// The caller may load a toolset into itself while it runs. Not published;
/// for discussion.
///
/// Spawning is declarative: a child's toolsets are fixed when it starts. This
/// interface is the separate, explicit power to change a running agent's own
/// toolsets. A world that does not import it cannot load anything, and a
/// spawned child never receives it in this version. A parent cannot load a
/// toolset into a child it spawned; it spawns a new child with different
/// toolsets instead.
///
/// A loaded toolset is a component with its own grants, started by the
/// implementer. The implementer offers its tool definitions through the
/// caller's own `cosmonic:agent/tools` import and dispatches calls to it, so
/// the caller carries no router; the same implementer must serve that import
/// for loading to work, which is why `tool-loading-provider` exports both.
/// Admission follows `spawn`: the toolset's reach must fit inside the
/// caller's ceiling, and anything beyond it goes to a grantor and returns
/// `needs-approval` with a digest over the caller's identity and the
/// `toolset-spec`. A grant is single use, as for `spawn`. Policy may ask
/// for approval even within the ceiling, since new code is new risk even
/// when its reach is not. A `toolset-spec` from a `tool-discovery` offer
/// loads unchanged, and its digest matches what the caller was shown.
///
/// The toolset joins the caller's session: its tools are offered from the
/// caller's next `tools.list` on, under aliases assigned by the same rules as
/// declared toolsets (own names first, namespaced only on a collision, never
/// renamed), and its calls are routed to the workload the implementer
/// started for it. A caller whose `tools` import the implementer does not
/// serve has nowhere to offer them, and `load` answers `unavailable`. An
/// implementer that does not enable loading answers `access-denied`.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface tool-loading {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use spawn-types.{toolset-spec, admission-error};

    /// A toolset loaded into the caller. Dropping it unloads the toolset: the
    /// implementer stops offering its tools and stops its component, and a
    /// later call to one of its aliases answers `unknown-tool`. Its aliases
    /// stay reserved for the session and are never given to another tool. A
    /// call in progress is cancelled cooperatively; its effects are not
    /// rolled back. The implementer also unloads every toolset when the
    /// caller's store ends, so a caller that wants a toolset across requests
    /// keeps the handle in an instance that outlives them.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    resource loaded-toolset {
        /// The aliases under which the caller's `tools` import offers this
        /// toolset's definitions, in the toolset's own order. An alias is the
        /// model-facing selection key and carries no authority. Chosen by the
        /// implementer (see `toolset-spec.expose-as`), unique in the caller's
        /// session, and stable for the toolset's lifetime in that session:
        /// loading another toolset never changes it.
        @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
        get-names: async func() -> list<string>;
    }

    /// Check the spec against the ceiling and policy, start the toolset, and
    /// offer its tools through the caller's `tools` import. Resolves once
    /// they are offered.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    load: async func(spec: toolset-spec) -> result<loaded-toolset, admission-error>;
}
